activities.content:
Look at the duplicated code in this traditional overloaded class:

```java
// ❌ Code Duplication Smell:
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
    String course;

    Student() {
        this.name = "Unknown";
        this.age = 18;
        this.course = "Undeclared";
    }

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = 18;          // Duplicated!
        this.course = "Undeclared"; // Duplicated!
    }
}
```
If you ever need to change the default age to 19, you have to edit it in multiple places. That is brittle.

### The Constructor Chaining Solution with `this(...)`:
We create ONE master constructor that does the real work, and have all other constructors chain into it!

```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
    String course;

    // 1. Master Constructor (does all actual field assignments)
    Student(String name, int age, String course) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
        this.course = course;
    }

    // 2. Chained Constructor: name only, delegates defaults for age & course
    Student(String name) {
        this(name, 18, "Undeclared"); // Calls Master Constructor!
    }

    // 3. Chained Constructor: no-arg, delegates defaults for everything
    Student() {
        this("Unknown"); // Chains to constructor 2 (which chains to master!)
    }
}
```

### ⚠️ The #1 Strict Rule of Constructor Chaining:
> **`this(...)` MUST be the FIRST line in the constructor!**

```java
Student(String name) {
    System.out.println("Setting up..."); // ❌ COMPILE ERROR!
    this(name, 18, "Undeclared");        // 'Constructor call must be first statement'
}
```
Java requires the object to be constructed first before executing any custom logging or side-effect statements.

activities.codeSnippet:
class Order {
    String orderId;
    String customer;
    double amount;

    // Master Constructor
    Order(String orderId, String customer, double amount) {
        this.orderId = orderId;
        this.customer = customer;
        this.amount = amount;
        System.out.println("Master constructor initialized order: " + this.orderId);
    }

    // Chained: Customer & Amount known, generate default order ID
    Order(String customer, double amount) {
        this("ORD-DEFAULT", customer, amount); // First statement!
    }

    // Chained: Empty order with zero amount
    Order() {
        this("Guest", 0.0); // Chains to (customer, amount)
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Creating order 1...");
        Order o1 = new Order();

        System.out.println("Customer: " + o1.customer + " | Amount: $" + o1.amount);
    }
}

class User {
    String username;
    String email;
    String membershipTier;

    // MASTER CONSTRUCTOR: Only this constructor writes to fields
    User(String username, String email, String membershipTier) {
        this.username = username;
        this.email = email;
        this.membershipTier = membershipTier;
        System.out.println("User created: " + this.username);
    }

    // Chained Constructor: username and email known, tier defaults to 'FREE'
    User(String username, String email) {
        this(username, email, "FREE");
    }

    // Chained Constructor: anonymous visitor
    User() {
        this("anonymous", "guest@site.com");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // All three chain into the master constructor:
        User u1 = new User();
        User u2 = new User("keerthi", "k@code.io");
        User u3 = new User("boss", "admin@corp.com", "PREMIUM");
    }
}

